---
import StoryBars from "../../components/StoryBars.astro";
import "../../styles.css";
import { viewerHref } from "../../lib/story-links";
import Head from "../_Head.astro";

const home = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/ui\/$/, "");
const repository = "https://github.com/simonvanlierde/brightway-benchmarks";
const single = "bench_lca.SingleScore.time_first_score";
const multi = "bench_lca.MultiScores.time_scores";
const carlo = "bench_lca.MonteCarlo.time_iterations";
const link = (group: string, bench: string, ...params: string[]) => viewerHref(home, group, bench, params);

// One score: benchmarks/findings.toml, "The solver sets the time for one score".
const oneScore = link("bw2calc-conda", single, "ecoinvent", "LCA");
const pipScore = link("bw2calc", single, "ecoinvent", "LCA");
// 30 products: published results of the latest bw2calc-conda run, in the viewer's MultiScores view.
const thirty = (variant: string) => link("bw2calc-conda", multi, "ecoinvent", "30", variant);
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <Head
      title="Your solver can make the same LCA ten times slower: Brightway benchmarks"
      description="One Brightway score on an ecoinvent-sized database takes 2.63 s, 10.0 s or 27.7 s, depending on which sparse solver you install."
      canonical="https://simonvanlierde.com/brightway-benchmarks/stories/solver-choice/"
      image="https://simonvanlierde.com/brightway-benchmarks/ui/story-solver-choice.png"
      imageAlt="Three bars for one Brightway score on an ecoinvent-sized database: UMFPACK 2.63 seconds, PyPardiso 10.0 seconds, SuperLU 27.7 seconds."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to the content</a>
    <header class="top">
      <div class="top-inner">
        <a class="story-brand" href={home}>brightway-benchmarks</a>
        <nav class="primary-nav" aria-label="Main">
          <a href={home}>Setup</a>
          <a href={`${home}?page=explorer`}>Benchmarks</a>
        </nav>
        <button type="button" class="theme-toggle" data-theme-toggle aria-label="Switch to dark theme" title="Switch to dark theme">
          <svg data-theme-icon="sun" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4"/><path d="M12 2v2m0 16v2M2 12h2m16 0h2M5 5l1.4 1.4m11.2 11.2L19 19M5 19l1.4-1.4M17.6 6.4 19 5"/></svg>
          <svg data-theme-icon="moon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20.9 13A9 9 0 0 1 11 3.1 9 9 0 1 0 20.9 13Z"/></svg>
        </button>
      </div>
    </header>
    <main class="story" id="main" tabindex="-1">
      <h1>Your solver can make the same LCA ten times slower</h1>
      <p class="lede">Every Brightway score ends in one large sparse linear system. Which package solves it depends on what happens to be installed, not on anything in your code. On an ecoinvent-sized database, that is the difference between 2.6 s and 28 s.</p>
      <p>The times come from one pinned benchmark machine, on one thread, with a synthetic database that has ecoinvent's size and shape. Each chart links to its benchmark's full history.</p>

      <h2>One score</h2>
      <p>bw2calc's <code>LCA</code> class builds the matrices, factorizes the technosphere, solves it and characterizes the result.</p>
      <StoryBars
        caption="Time to the first score"
        href={oneScore}
        rows={[
          { label: "UMFPACK, conda-forge build on OpenBLAS", seconds: 2.63, tone: "fast" },
          { label: "PyPardiso, MKL", seconds: 10.0, tone: "mid" },
          { label: "SuperLU, SciPy's default", seconds: 27.7, tone: "base" },
        ]}
      />
      <p>With nothing extra installed, you get SciPy's SuperLU, the slowest bar. Install PyPardiso and bw2calc uses it automatically; bw_timex installs it for you on Linux x86_64 and 64-bit Windows. bw2calc picks UMFPACK only when PyPardiso is absent, so to get the fastest bar, install scikit-umfpack from conda-forge and leave PyPardiso out.</p>
      <p>The conda-forge part matters. The pip build of scikit-umfpack runs on reference BLAS and takes about 9 s, no faster than PyPardiso (<a href={pipScore}>the pip build's history</a>).</p>

      <h2>Thirty products at once</h2>
      <p><code>MultiLCA</code> scores 30 products against three impact methods in one call.</p>
      <StoryBars
        caption="MultiLCA, 30 products"
        href={thirty("MultiLCA")}
        rows={[
          { label: "PyPardiso, MKL", seconds: 10.6, tone: "fast" },
          { label: "SuperLU, with or without scikit-umfpack", seconds: 28.7, tone: "base" },
        ]}
      />
      <p>Here scikit-umfpack does nothing. <code>MultiLCA</code> hands SciPy all 30 demands as one matrix, and SciPy uses UMFPACK only for a single right-hand side. The SuperLU bar is the same with or without it, and PyPardiso is 2.7 times faster.</p>
      <p>If you only need the scores, <code>FastScoresOnlyMultiLCA</code> solves one product at a time and UMFPACK is back in front: 3.56 s, against 10.5 s for PyPardiso (<a href={thirty("forward")}>the forward-direction history</a>). It does not run on SuperLU at all.</p>

      <h2>Monte Carlo</h2>
      <p>Each draw samples new matrix values, so the solver starts over every time. Ten draws with <code>LCA</code> and PyPardiso take 99.3 s, about ten single scores (<a href={link("bw2calc-conda", carlo, "ecoinvent", "10", "LCA")}>the Monte Carlo history</a>). The published runs have no UMFPACK or SuperLU times for this, so the page does not guess them.</p>

      <h2>What to install</h2>
      <p>For one score at a time: scikit-umfpack from conda-forge, without PyPardiso. For many products in one go: PyPardiso, or <code>FastScoresOnlyMultiLCA</code> with UMFPACK if you only need the scores.</p>
      <p><a href={home}>Setup</a> has install sets that passed their checks on each platform. <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/docs/faster.md`}>Make your Brightway faster</a> lists other install choices and calls that change the time, each with its measured gain. The <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/docs/method.md`}>method</a> describes the database and how noisy the timings are.</p>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  .top-inner > .theme-toggle {
    margin-left: auto;
  }
  .story-brand {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 650;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .story {
    max-width: 52rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 40px 24px 64px;
  }
  .story h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }
  .story h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    margin: 32px 0 10px;
  }
  .story p {
    margin: 12px 0;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .story .lede {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
</style>
